# dry-copy: ci/shard-packing-cases — scala-build's TestShards.pack and every reimplementation of it are held to these cases; `dev repo copies` checks every copy (ISS-120027)
#
# The greedy packing a split build places its parts with (scala-build's `TestShards.pack`), one case a line:
#
#   <label>=<seconds> ... | <shard count> | <labels of shard 1> ; <labels of shard 2> ...
#
# The items are placed longest first (equal seconds in label order), each onto the least-loaded shard
# so far (equal loads onto the earliest). The expected shards are the ones that hold any, longest
# first (equal totals in the order of their first labels), each listing its items longest first.
# A blank line and a line starting with `#` hold no case.

# One shard holds everything, longest first.
a=1 b=2 c=3 | 1 | c b a

# More shards than items: the shards left empty are not filed.
a=5 b=3 | 4 | a ; b

# Longest first, each onto the least-loaded shard; a tie in load goes to the earliest shard.
a=300 b=200 c=100 d=100 | 2 | a d ; b c

# Equal seconds are placed in label order.
b=10 a=10 | 2 | a ; b

# Shards of equal totals are ordered by their first labels, not by the order they were filled.
z=5 a=3 b=2 | 2 | a b ; z

# Greedy, not optimal: 3 3 2 2 2 packs 7 and 5, where 6 and 6 exists.
a=3 b=3 c=2 d=2 e=2 | 2 | a c e ; b d

# Fractional seconds.
a=12.5 b=0.25 c=12.25 | 2 | a ; c b

# A larger build: every shard receives work before any receives a second item.
a=90 b=80 c=70 d=60 e=50 f=40 g=30 | 3 | a f g ; b e ; c d

# An item of no seconds still lands on a shard, after every longer one.
a=0 b=4 c=4 | 2 | b a ; c
# dry-copy-end
